artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a five-dimensional bulk spacetime containing a four-dimensional Robertson–Walker 3-brane. Assume the bulk matter content is a perfect fluid, characterized by an energy density ρ and pressure p with a linear equation of state p = wρ (either described from the bulk viewpoint or induced from a brane fluid description). The 3-brane carries an induced Robertson–Walker metric with scale factor a(t) and spatial curvature k ∈ {−1, 0, +1}, and is embedded in the bulk in a Z₂-symmetric way.

Determine whether there exists a bulk solution of the five-dimensional Einstein equations sourced by such a perfect fluid that simultaneously satisfies all three of the following conditions:
(i) the bulk geometry is regular, i.e. free of curvature singularities at any finite proper distance from the brane;
(ii) gravity is localized on the brane, in the sense that the integral defining the induced four-dimensional Planck mass (the warp-factor integral transverse to the brane) is finite;
(iii) the null energy condition holds throughout the bulk, i.e. T_{MN} k^M k^N ≥ 0 for every null vector k^M.

Equivalently: prove or disprove that, for Robertson–Walker branes with a single perfect-fluid bulk, conditions (i)–(iii) can be met simultaneously.

